# Audit results and detailed explanations, part J (q541-q600). Format: see explain_a.py.
E = {}
def e(qid, stem, exp, opts=None, q=None):
    E[qid] = dict(stem=stem, exp=exp, opts=opts, q=q)

e("q541", "Variance analysis compares ___ results", "Variance analysis compares planned (budgeted or standard) results with actual results: variance = actual - planned. The size and direction of each difference show where performance departed from the plan. It isn't a comparison with competitors or between gross and net figures.")
e("q542", "After finding a large labor variance on a project", "Variance analysis is only useful if it leads to action. The manager should investigate why labor ran over, such as underestimated tasks, scope creep, inexperienced staff or equipment problems, then adjust the plan or future estimates. Ignoring it or firing everyone doesn't fix the cause.")
e("q543", "A marketing budget shows a favorable variance because", "Spending under budget looks favorable, but here it happened because planned email campaigns were never sent, so the work that drives sales wasn't done. Future sales may suffer. Every variance, favorable or not, should be investigated for its cause.")
e("q544", "Tracking variances regularly helps managers", "Budgetary control means using the budget as a standard and regularly comparing actual results against it, so problems are caught and corrected early instead of at year-end. Variance tracking is the main tool. It doesn't replace planning, raise prices or remove fixed costs.")
e("q545", "Cost accounting budgets help managers mainly by serving", "Cost budgets set the expected cost of production, and comparing actual costs with them shows whether departments controlled spending. That makes them a financial control tool for evaluating performance. They don't replace financial statements, tax returns or marketing plans.")
e("q546", "In the management functions, using a cost budget to tell", "Directing is guiding people's work toward goals. A cost budget directs activity by telling each department how much it can spend and on what, so their effort and resources line up with the plan. Recruiting is a staffing task, and advertising is marketing.")
e("q547", "A production budget that shows total costs of making goods", "A production cost budget brings together direct materials, direct labor and manufacturing overhead, so management sees the total cost of producing goods. That's essential for pricing, planning cash needs and checking profitability. It doesn't set competitors' prices or calculate dividends.")
e("q548", "A cost that rises and falls in direct proportion to production", "Variable costs change in direct proportion to production volume: make twice as many units and packaging costs roughly double. Fixed costs, like rent and salaries, stay the same in the short run regardless of volume. Cost budgets separate the two to plan accurately.")
e("q549", "Comparing actual production costs to the cost budget", "Comparing actual production costs with the cost budget reveals variances, places where spending was higher or lower than planned. Investigating them uncovers waste, price changes or process problems, so the company can improve efficiency.")
e("q550", "Cost budgets help define roles because they", "Each line of a cost budget is assigned to a specific manager who's responsible for staying within it. That clarifies who is accountable for which costs and gives each manager authority over their area. It increases accountability rather than removing it.")
e("q551", "Using a cost budget to judge how well a production manager", "A cost budget sets a benchmark for what costs should be. Comparing a manager's actual costs with it shows how well they controlled spending, so budgets are used to evaluate performance. Fair evaluation also considers whether the costs were within the manager's control.")
e("q552", "Assigning a share of the building's electricity bill", "Cost allocation divides a shared, indirect cost, like a building's electricity, among departments using an allocation base that reflects use, here square footage. A department with 25% of the space gets 25% of the bill. It doesn't change the total cost, only how it's distributed.")
e("q553", "Which cost is most likely to be allocated among departments", "Electricity for the whole building serves every department, and there's usually no meter per department, so it can't be traced directly and must be allocated. Raw materials for one product, a commission on one sale and wages of one product's assembler are direct costs.")
e("q554", "Costs are allocated when", "Allocation is needed when a cost is shared and can't be traced to or controlled by a single department or product, such as rent, utilities, IT or a CEO's salary. Costs that belong entirely to one product are simply assigned directly.")
e("q555", "A bank divides its call center costs among branches", "The allocation base is the measure used to divide a shared cost. Here it's the number of customers each branch serves, a sensible choice because more customers likely means more calls. A good base reflects what actually drives the cost.")
e("q556", "Allocation moves ___ costs to departments", "Allocation assigns indirect (shared) costs to departments or products, where they're added to the direct costs already traced there. Direct costs plus allocated indirect costs give the full cost, which managers need for pricing and profitability decisions.")
e("q557", "Why does cost allocation matter for pricing", "If overhead isn't allocated, a product's cost looks lower than it really is, and its price might cover materials and labor but not its share of rent, utilities and administration. Over time that loses money. Allocation helps set prices that cover all costs and earn a profit.")
e("q558", "As the economy moves from recession into recovery", "Investors price stocks on expected future earnings. As the economy moves from recession toward recovery, they anticipate rising sales and profits, so they tend to buy stocks, often before the recovery shows up in jobs data. That's why markets often bottom before the economy does.")
e("q559", "Which institution lends to developing countries", "The World Bank Group provides loans, grants and expertise to governments of developing countries for projects like roads, schools, health care and clean water, with the goal of reducing poverty. The FDIC insures U.S. deposits, and credit unions and brokerages serve individual customers.")
e("q560", "Stocks of small companies that aren't listed on a major", "Securities not listed on a major exchange like the NYSE or Nasdaq trade over the counter (OTC), directly between dealers through networks such as the OTC Markets Group. OTC stocks are often smaller, less liquid and riskier, with lighter disclosure requirements.")
e("q561", "Which finance career focuses on evaluating risks", "An insurance underwriter evaluates applicants' risk, such as health, driving record or property condition, and decides whether to issue a policy and at what premium. Investment bank underwriters do something similar for new securities. Tellers, planners and collectors have different roles.")
e("q562", "A client is confused by a fund's prospectus", "Part of a finance professional's responsibility is making sure clients understand what they're buying. A responsible advisor explains both the benefits and the drawbacks, including risks and fees, in plain language so the client can make an informed decision. Explaining only the benefits is misleading.")
e("q563", "An analyst needs a list of only clients in Springfield", "A database query with filters, such as city = Springfield AND balance > $250,000, instantly returns exactly the matching records. Retyping records is slow and error-prone, a pie chart displays data rather than selecting it, and a word-processing template isn't a database tool.")
e("q564", "Which ratio type shows how efficiently a company uses", "Asset utilization (turnover) ratios, such as total asset turnover = sales / total assets, show how efficiently a company uses its assets to generate sales. Higher turnover means more sales per dollar of assets. Liquidity ratios measure ability to pay short-term bills.")
e("q565", "A bank compares the debt-to-income ratios of loan applicants", "Credit standards set the maximum risk a lender will accept. A 52% DTI means more than half the applicant's income already goes to debt, well above the bank's 43% limit, so the bank will likely decline the loan or restructure it, for example as a smaller amount or with a cosigner.")
e("q566", "Financial globalization has been driven partly by", "Financial globalization grew as the internet made cross-border transactions instant and cheap, and as countries reduced capital controls and deregulated markets. Money can now move around the world in seconds. Banning trade, fixing exchange rates or closing exchanges would do the opposite.")
e("q567", "Under ERM, organizations are encouraged to view some risks", "Enterprise risk management treats risk as two-sided: some risks, such as entering a new market or launching a product, can create value if managed well. ERM ties risk to strategy instead of seeing it only as something to avoid or insure.")
e("q568", "Installing security cameras in a bank branch is a form", "Security cameras deter theft and help catch thieves, lowering both the likelihood and the cost of losses. That's risk control (reduction). Risk transfer would be buying insurance, and risk retention would be accepting losses without action.")
e("q569", "Why does ethical financial-information management make", "Investors and lenders need to trust a company's numbers before committing money. Accurate, honest financial information builds that trust and lowers perceived risk, making it easier and cheaper to raise money. Ethics can't guarantee a higher stock price or remove business risk.")
e("q570", "An estate passing directly to a surviving spouse", "The unlimited marital deduction lets a person leave any amount to a U.S.-citizen spouse free of federal estate tax. Taxes may apply later when the surviving spouse's estate passes to others, above the exemption ($15 million per person in 2026).")
e("q571", "Who benefits from good corporate governance", "Good governance balances the interests of all stakeholders: shareholders get honest management and better returns, employees get fair treatment and stable jobs, customers get trustworthy products and communities get responsible corporate citizens. It's not designed to benefit just the CEO.")
e("q572", "An internal audit function in a well-governed company", "Internal auditors independently test whether internal controls work and whether policies and laws are followed, then report findings to management and the board's audit committee. That gives leaders assurance that risks are managed. Pricing, advertising and hiring belong to other functions.")
e("q573", "A major risk that comes with storing financial data online", "Data stored online can be reached from anywhere, which also makes it a target for hackers. A cybersecurity breach can expose sensitive financial and customer data, leading to losses, fines and lost trust. Faster reporting, lower printing costs and easier collaboration are benefits, not risks.")
e("q574", "A business owner tracks actual spending against the budget", "Tracking (monitoring) compares actual spending with the budget regularly, monthly here, so overspending is caught early and the plan can be adjusted. A budget that isn't tracked is just a guess. Budgeting software makes this comparison quick.")
e("q575", "Getting a paycheck deposited a week earlier and investing", "Time is a key driver of the time value of money: the sooner money is invested, the longer it earns interest and compounds. Even small head starts add up over many years. Interest rates don't change by day of the week, and investing doesn't avoid taxes.")
e("q576", "Combining the financial statements of a parent company", "Consolidated financial statements combine the assets, liabilities, revenue and expenses of a parent company and its subsidiaries into one set, as if they were a single company. Investors see the whole group's financial picture. Transactions between the companies are eliminated.")
e("q577", "Software that reads new regulations, flags which ones apply", "Regulatory change management tools track new and updated rules, identify which apply to the firm, and assign tasks to update policies, procedures and training. Regulations change constantly, so technology helps firms stay compliant without missing anything.")
e("q578", "In an agency relationship, the agent must", "In an agency relationship, the agent acts on behalf of the principal, such as an employee for an employer or a broker for a client, and owes fiduciary duties: loyalty, obedience to lawful instructions, reasonable care and accounting for money. The agent must put the principal's interests first.")
e("q579", "Laws that limit pollution and protect natural resources", "Environmental law protects air, water, land and wildlife from harmful business and human activity, through laws like the Clean Air Act and Clean Water Act enforced by the EPA. Businesses must comply or face fines and cleanup costs. Contract law governs agreements.")
e("q580", "Rules that limit how many fish a company can catch", "Conservation laws manage natural resources so they aren't used up, for example fishing quotas, hunting seasons and limits on logging. They protect future supplies for businesses and communities. Antitrust law addresses competition, and labor law addresses employment.")
e("q581", "A disease kills many orange trees, so fewer oranges reach", "When supply falls and demand stays the same, there are fewer oranges for the same number of buyers. Prices rise until enough buyers drop out that the smaller supply is enough. Rising prices ration scarce goods and signal growers to produce more.")
e("q582", "A risk that could produce either a gain or a loss", "Speculative risk can result in a gain, a loss or no change, such as launching a new product, investing or expanding. Pure risk can only result in a loss or no loss, such as a fire or theft, which is why pure risks are generally insurable and speculative risks are not.")
e("q583", "A snowstorm closes a store for three days", "Natural risks come from nature: storms, floods, earthquakes and fires caused by lightning. A snowstorm closing a store is a natural risk. Human risks come from people (theft, errors), and market risks from economic conditions.")
e("q584", "Assigning each worker one repeated task on an assembly line", "Division of labor (specialization) breaks work into tasks and assigns each worker one task, which they repeat and become fast and skilled at. It usually raises productivity. Job enrichment adds variety and responsibility, and job rotation moves workers between tasks.")
e("q585", "A drawback of very narrow job specialization", "Doing the same small task over and over can become boring, lowering morale and increasing turnover. Very narrow skills may also become obsolete if technology automates that task. Companies counter this with job rotation and enrichment.")
e("q586", "When doing business with clients from another culture", "Learning a client's customs, such as greetings, communication style, attitudes toward time and decision-making, and communicating clearly shows respect and prevents misunderstandings. Assuming everyone shares your customs, speaking fast or using slang can confuse or offend.")
e("q587", "A gesture that's friendly in one country can be insulting", "Cultural and social environments, including values, customs, language and nonverbal communication, differ across countries. A thumbs-up or an 'OK' sign means different things in different places, so international business requires cultural awareness.")
e("q588", "A tax that takes a larger percentage of income from people", "A progressive tax takes a larger percentage of income as income rises; the federal income tax, with rates from 10% to 37%, is progressive. A regressive tax takes a larger share from lower-income people (sales taxes tend to be), and a proportional tax uses one rate for everyone.")
e("q589", "Organizing a bank's departments around retail customers", "Organizing by customer type groups work around the kinds of customers served, such as retail, business and wealthy clients, so each department can specialize in their needs. Businesses can also organize by function, product or geography.")
e("q590", "Whatever its structure, the organizational design", "A for-profit business's structure exists to help it reach its goals, ultimately earning a profit by meeting customer needs efficiently. Good design clarifies roles, speeds decisions and puts resources where they create the most value. More paperwork isn't the goal.")
e("q591", "A main benefit of regular staff communication between managers", "Regular two-way communication gives employees chances to give feedback and suggest improvements, and gives managers early warning of problems. It improves morale and decisions. It doesn't eliminate meetings, guarantee promotions or replace training.")
e("q592", "An informational message to employees about a new expense", "An informational message's purpose is to make sure readers understand facts they need, here the new policy: what changed, who it applies to, when it starts and how to follow it. It should be clear and complete. Persuading, entertaining or criticizing would be different purposes.")
e("q593", "Which behavior helps a group discussion", "Asking questions to clarify others' comments shows you're listening, prevents misunderstandings and keeps the discussion productive. Blurting out statements, interrupting to change the subject and checking your phone all disrupt the group.")
e("q594", "In a group discussion, a member who proposes a direction", "Proposing a direction or plan for the group is a positive group role, sometimes called initiating, that sets the group's course and moves the discussion forward. Blocking, withdrawing and dominating are negative roles that hurt group progress.")
e("q595", "Before using an article's data in a report, you should", "Before relying on information, check its credibility: who the author is and what qualifies them, who published it, whether it cites sources and how current it is. Using unreliable data can lead to wrong conclusions. Length, pictures or being online don't make a source valid.")
e("q596", "Which is a sign that written material is likely reliable", "Reliable material usually comes from an author with relevant professional qualifications, published by a reputable source, with cited evidence you can check. Anonymous posts, unattributed content and emotional formatting like many exclamation points are warning signs.")
e("q597", "When a colleague disagrees with your proposal in a meeting", "Defending an idea objectively means acknowledging the other person's concerns respectfully and responding with facts and reasoning that support your view. That keeps the discussion professional and persuasive. Raising your voice, attacking the person or refusing to discuss it undermines your credibility.")
e("q598", "How should you prepare for a staff meeting", "Preparing, by reading the agenda and documents and doing any research ahead of time, lets you contribute useful ideas and make decisions efficiently. It respects everyone's time. Arriving late or bringing unrelated work signals you don't value the meeting.")
e("q599", "During a staff meeting, it's appropriate to ask a question", "Asking a question when you need a point clarified keeps everyone on the same page and prevents mistakes later. Changing the subject, asking about things covered in the agenda you didn't read or interrupting someone mid-sentence is disruptive.")
e("q600", "To show how a company's revenue changed each month", "A pie chart shows how a whole divides into parts at one point in time, so it's ideal for a portfolio's mix, such as 60% stocks, 30% bonds and 10% cash. Line graphs show trends over time, bar charts compare amounts across categories and organizational charts show reporting structure. Use percentages that add to 100%.",
  q="To show a client what percentage of her portfolio is in stocks, bonds and cash right now, the best graphic aid is a",
  opts=["pie chart", "line graph", "organizational chart", "Gantt chart"])
